      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_06_Sugars_and_Honey",
      "oe:isAggregate": false,
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "sugar prepared from the sap of the *Acer saccharinum*, which is largely made for home consumption by the settlers in parts of the United States and British North American Provinces: usually it is of a dark treacle colour, as it does not granulate readily; but it is occasionally refined and bleached. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 252]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "North American forest-derived sweetener (Acer saccharum); assigned oe:landBasis 'terrestrial_forest' to distinguish sugarbush forest harvest from arable cane/beet cropland.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
