      "oe:theodoridisBucket": "raw_materials",
      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_21_Hides_Skins_Furskins_Raw",
      "oe:isAggregate": false,
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a name generally applied by tanners to the pelts of small animals, as sheep, goats, seals, dogs, &c. The skins of the marine mammalia, as the seal, and whale, and the porpoise, when properly manufactured, are stronger than those of land animals. In 1855, we imported 3,600,780 skins of the lamb, goat, sheep, kid, and seal; and 653,961 ewts. of hides, worth together about £2,500,000. This was exclusive of furs. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 359]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
        {
          "@id": "https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n359"
