      "oe:ghostAcresDomain": "fats_oils_soap",
      "oe:landBasis": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "*Hemsl.*, *Kew Bull.*, 1906, 117, 120; Hooker, *IC. Pl.*, tt. 2801, 2802 (confused with *A. cordata* by most authors); EUPHORBIACEÆ or SPURGEWORTS. A small tree, native of China but successfully acclimatised in the Southern Shan States (between 1,500 and 3,000 feet in altitude) and to some extent also in Burma, Assam, Sikkim and Nepal. Frequently met with near Buddhist monaster [Sir George Watt, The Commercial Products of India (1908), scan p. 58]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0012 (Seeds, Nuts & Kernels for Oil Unenumerated). Attested in: Sir George Watt, The Commercial Products of India (1908), scan p. 58.",
