      "oe:landBasis": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "the fluid which circulates in the heart and blood-vessels of animals, which is used for many commercial purposes: it is stirred and run into casks for the use of sugar refiners; it is made into animal charcoal; congealed, it is sold to calico-printers for dyeing Turkey red, and is chemically prepared for printers' use. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 53]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 53, Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562).",
