      "oe:landBasis": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a viscid silky fibre produced by the wing-shell mollusc (*Pinna angusta*) in the Mediterranean and West Indies, &c. The filaments are extremely fine and strong, and often reach 3 feet in length. The colour, which is a reddish brown, never fades. Stockings, gloves, and other articles have been woven of it, but more as a curiosity than for use. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 70]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0822 (Vegetable Fibres Unenumerated (incl. Esparto, Cordage & Brush Fibres)). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 70, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 190.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
