      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a sort of isinglass made with the sturgeon's bladder, imported from Archangel. The chief use of it is for clarifying wine; but it is also used by dyers. See **ISINGLASS**. **CARMELINE, or CARMENIA WOOL**, (GER. *Karamenirche oder Kirmansche Wolle*. DU. *Kirmansche Wol*. FR. *Laine de Carmenie*. IT. *Lana di Caramania*. SP. *Lana de Caramenia*. PORT. *Laa de Caramania*,) is the  [Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 203]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 203, P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 82.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
