          "@value": "natural red 4",
          "@language": "en"
        },
        {
          "@value": "Polish cochineal",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a beautiful lako pigment, a fine bright crimson inclining to scarlet, formed by a combination of cochineal, alumina, and oxide of tin. In consequence of being more transparent than other colours, it is chiefly used for miniature painting, artificial-flower tinting, and water-colour drawing. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 82]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 82, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 204.",
        "@language": "en"
