      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "croton eluteria",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Cascarilla bark comes from plants of the Croton species, which are cultivated in Caribbean and shipped from the Bahamas. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croton_eluteria) Cascarilla is used as a Tonic and as a Carminative. It can also be combined with cinchona to make the mixture more agreeable to the stomach. It is used in the treatment of dyspepsia, flatulent colic, dysentery, and gangrenous thrush in children. [Danika Drugs Project: British Pharmacopoeia (1867) / Savory (1865)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: Danika Drugs.xlsx (British Pharmacopoeia 1867 / Savory 1865), Danika Drugs.xlsx (SKOS Sheet), 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 85, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 206. Research note: Stomachic.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
