      "oe:landBasis": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Of this plant, which is a shrub about six feet high, extensively grown for its farinaceous root, there are several species, nearly all natives of America, principally of Brazil, whence it derives one of its common names of Manihot or Mandioc. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854, Project Gutenberg #15191)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0408 (Spices Unenumerated (incl. Condiments & Minor Foods)). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854, Project Gutenberg #15191), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 86.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
