        "@id": "htc:0122"
      },
      "oe:groundingStatus": "document_attested_local",
      "oe:ghostAcresDomain": "drugs_botanicals_chemicals",
      "oe:landBasis": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Java pepper",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Recorded in the Pharmacopeia and the Compendium as Cubebs, it consists of the dried unripe fruit of the plant Cubeba officinalis, which is cultivated in Java. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubeb) Cubeb is listed in the Compendium as a Diuretic that is also slightly purgative. It can be used in the treatment of gonorrhoea, gleets, fluor albus, urinary irritations, and gravel. [Danika Drugs Project: British Pharmacopoeia (1867) / Savory (1865)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: Danika Drugs.xlsx (British Pharmacopoeia 1867 / Savory 1865), Danika Drugs.xlsx (SKOS Sheet), 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab). Research note: Find under Cubebs; not much on Google, but it does come up in a sentence with other spies (ginger, mace, nutmeg); likely another spelling for Cubeb, or Java peper..",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    },
