      "oe:landBasis": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "the principal silver coin and money of account in Greece, worth 6d. sterling. In Britain, the term dram is applied to two weights; in apothecaries weight, to the eighth part of the troy ounce, or 60 troy grains; and to the one-sixteenth part of the avoirdupois ounce, or 27 11-32nd troy grains. The latter, however, is seldom used. In medicine, the drachm weight is expressed in p [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 143]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 143, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 275.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
        {
          "@id": "https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n143"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n275"
        }
      ]
    },
