      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_53_Dyeing_Tanning_Colouring",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a well-known hard strong yellow dye-wood. The old fustic of commerce is obtained from the *Maclura tinctoria*, a tree of South America. The wood is admirably adapted for the fellos of carriage and eart wheels. The young fustic of commerce is procured from *Rhus* [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 178]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 178, Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562).",
