      "oe:landBasis": "mineral",
      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_51_52_Chemicals",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a well-known explosive substance prepared from nitre, charcoal, and sulphur: the compound consists usually of 75 parts of saltpetre, 10 of sulphur and 15 of charcoal, mixed and reduced to a fine powder, then granulated and dried; but the proportion of the ingredients varies in different countries, and according to the purpose for which it is to be used. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 195]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0010 (Other Articles). Attested in: 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 195, Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562). Research note: Too processed?.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
