      "oe:landBasis": "terrestrial_crop",
      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_05_Vegetables_and_Fruit",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a name for the *Andropogon citratum* of Decandolle. From its fragrant smell and pleasant taste, it is in common use in the West Indies as a substitute for Chinese tea, and is frequently employed as a sudorific in febrile diseases. The white succulent centre or pith of the leaves is used in India to give an agreeable flavour to curries. It is largely cultivated in Ceylon and the [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 238]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0124 (Oil & Essential Oil Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 238, P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 480.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
