      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Linseed oil is obtained by pressing the seeds of the flax plant (it is also sometimes called flaxseed oil). Its uses include acting as a pigment binder, plasticiser or hardener in putty, wood finisher, along with a nutrition supplement in food. Because linseed oil quickly oxidises, it can spontaneously combust when left unattended and soaked into rags. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linseed_oil) Because of its heatin [Danika Drugs Project: British Pharmacopoeia (1867) / Savory (1865)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: Danika Drugs.xlsx (British Pharmacopoeia 1867 / Savory 1865), 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 242, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 586.",
