      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "muskrat",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a burrowing animal, the *Fiber zibethicus*, commonly called the musk-rat, native of North America, sought for its skin, the fur resembling that of the beaver. It is chiefly used for hat-making. Rather more than a million skins are annually imported, which are for the most part sent to the Continent. \\* [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 268]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0071 (Furs, Skins & Marine Products Unenumerated). Attested in: 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 268, P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 487.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
