      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Our imports now are seldom more than 30,000 tons each year. **OARSMAN**, a species of tricycle so called. **OBOLUS**, an old Roman weight of about half a scruple. **OBOVATE**, inversely egg shaped. **OBSOLETE**, old-fashioned, suppressed, or out of date. **OUST** (German), fruit. **OCARINA**, a musical instrument played with the mouth. **OCUBA**, a kind of wax imported from Bra [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 489]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0041 (Tanning Barks & Extracts Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 489, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 678.",
        "@language": "en"
