      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "an argillaceous earth of different colours, which, when finely ground, is used as a pigment; a name given to the oxides of various metals. Red ochre is a form of specular iron ore; brown ochre a variety of hematite. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 275]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 275, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 679.",
