      "oe:landBasis": "terrestrial_crop",
      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_11_Beverages",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a weaver's term in Scotland for twenty splits, or threads, in plain linen work; in England it is termed a beer; a door-keeper; a carrier of burdens; a termounted malt liquor, which is distinguished from ale or beer by its darker colour, arising in some degree from an incipient charring of the malt, which also imparts an agreeable bitter taste to it. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 309]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 309, Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562).",
