      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_53_Dyeing_Tanning_Colouring",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "(GER. *Berlinerblau*. DU. *Berlyns Blaaww*. DA. *Berliner Blaat*. SW. *Berliner blätt*. FR. *Bleu de Prusse*. IT. *Azurro Prussiano ò di Berlino*. SP. *Azul de Prussia*. PORT. *Azul de Allemanha ò de Prussia*. RUS. *Lasor Berlinskaja*. LAT. *Cæruleum Berolinense*.) Iron is the basis of this fine blue pigment, called, from the place where it was first discovered, Berlin, or Prus [Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 816]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 816, Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562).",
