      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_53_Dyeing_Tanning_Colouring",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "quercus velutina bark",
          "@language": "en"
        },
        {
          "@value": "eastern black oak bark",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a name for the black oak (*Quercus tinctoria*) of the United States, the wood of which is valuable for building, and for cutting into staves: the bark is used for tanning, while the cellular integument is extensively employed in dyeing wool, silk, and paper hangings, and forms an important article of export from Philadelphia. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 321]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 321, Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
