      "oe:landBasis": "terrestrial_crop",
      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_22_Oil_Seeds_and_Oleaginous_Fruits",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "(GER. *Rübsaamen*, *Rübs-* *sen. Du. Raapzaad. DA. Roefrö. Sw. Roffrö. FR. Graine de navette, ou ravette. IT. Ravizzone. SP. Nabina. PORT. Semente de nabos, Nabica. RUS. Rüpnoe semja. POL. Rzepnica,*) is the seed of the *napus stativa*, or long-rooted, narrow-leaved *rapa*, called in English navew, and reckoned by Linæus among the *brassicas* or cabbage-kind. This plant is cult [Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 843]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0010 (Other Articles). Attested in: 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 843.",
