      "oe:landBasis": "terrestrial_forest",
      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_24_Cork_and_Wood",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "an East Indian dye-wood, the produce of the *Pterocarpus santalinus*, a hard heavy wood imported from Madras and Calcutta. In 1856 117,901 Indian maunds (86,426 tons) of this wood were shipped from Madras. Besides its use as a dye-wood it is employed as the basis of various dentifrice mixtures. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 328]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 328.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
