      "oe:ghostAcresDomain": "tannins_leather",
      "oe:landBasis": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a much-prized fur; that from the Russian marten is so costly that it is rarely used, except by monarchs and high functionaries. The hair of the tail is used for artists' brushes or pencils. The American sable has a fur, varying in colour from tawny to black; it is often coloured to imitate Russian. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 336]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0071 (Furs, Skins & Marine Products Unenumerated). Attested in: 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 336, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 880.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
