      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "is a liquid condiment, or sauce, imported chiefly from China. It is prepared with a species of white haricots, wheat flour, common salt, and water; in the proportions respectively of 50, 60, 50, and 250 pounds. The haricots are washed, and boiled in water till they become so soft as to yield to the fingers. They are then laid in a flat dish to cool, and kneaded along with the f [Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0408 (Spices Unenumerated (incl. Condiments & Minor Foods)). Attested in: 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
