      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "flows from the twigs and the trunk of the _Liquidambar styraciflua_, a tree which grows in Louisiana, Virginia, and Mexico. Liquidamber, as this resin is also called, is a brown or ash-gray substance, of the consistence of turpentine, which dries up rapidly, has an agreeable smell, like benzoin, and a bitterish, sharp, burning taste. It dissolves in four parts of alcohol, and a [Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562), Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 945.",
