      },
      "oe:groundingStatus": "document_attested_local",
      "oe:ghostAcresDomain": "drugs_botanicals_chemicals",
      "oe:landBasis": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "is an alkaline base, extracted from the _Strychnos nux vomica_, _Strychnos ignatia_, and the _Upas tiente_; which has been employed in medicine by some of the poison doctors, but is of no use in any of the arts. When introduced into the stomach, strychnia acts with fearful energy, causing lock-jaw immediately, and the death of the animal in a very short time. Half a grain, blow [Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 376.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
