      "oe:landBasis": "terrestrial_forest",
      "oe:sitcCode": "SITC4_29_Crude_Animal_Vegetable_Materials",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "the dried and chopped leaves and shoots of the *Rhus cortaria*, a shrub growing in Southern Europe. When ground to powder in a mill, sumach is largely used for dyeing and tanning. The colouring matter is yellow. Our imports, average about 18,000 tons per annum. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 379]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 379, P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 510.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
