      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "verdigres",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "the acetate of copper; obtained by exposing thin plates of copper, for some time, to the action of the air or more of the wine-presses in the South of France. Besides the French verdigris, there are green distilled, common, and crystallized verdigris. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 411]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs & Dye Woods Unenumerated). Attested in: Danika Drugs.xlsx (British Pharmacopoeia 1867 / Savory 1865), 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 411, Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562). Research note: Subacetate of copper of commerce, used in pigment, and chemical testing. Not much else for drugs..",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource": [
        {
