      },
      "oe:groundingStatus": "document_attested_local",
      "oe:ghostAcresDomain": "fats_oils_soap",
      "oe:landBasis": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a name in British Guiana for the seeds of the *Sesamum orientale*, which, when parched and pounded, make a rich soup. Their use as an oil-seed has been noticed under Gingelle. The fresh leaves of this plant, when broken and slightly bristled in cold water, form a mucilaginous drink, used as a domestic remedy in dysentery. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 416]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0012 (Seeds, Nuts & Kernels for Oil Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 416.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
